  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)-1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,3+1)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(4))=(2,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $4$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-4+4)=(2,0).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)-1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(2)-1=3\\,\u27f9\\,f(2)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+4 \\\\  & =-4+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,0)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+4.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
